Du sade:
What does this mean? postgresql@17 is keg-only, which means it was not symlinked into /opt/homebrew
ChatGPT sade:
This message comes from Homebrew, and it simply means:

✅ Meaning
“postgresql@17 is keg-only”
→ Homebrew installed PostgreSQL 17 but did not create symlinks like:

/opt/homebrew/bin/psql
pointing to the version you installed.

So the software is installed, but not “linked” into the main Homebrew prefix, to avoid conflicts with other versions.

❓ Why does Homebrew do this?
A formula is keg-only when:

Another version is meant to be the default (e.g., postgresql@16).

Symlinking could conflict with system tools or other installed versions.

Homebrew wants you to explicitly choose the version to use.

🔍 How to use PostgreSQL 17 anyway
Option 1 — Run it from its full path
You can always call it directly:

/opt/homebrew/opt/postgresql@17/bin/psql
Option 2 — Add it to PATH
If you want this version available as psql:

echo 'export PATH="/opt/homebrew/opt/postgresql@17/bin:$PATH"' >> ~/.zshrc
source ~/.zshrc
Now psql --version should show PostgreSQL 17.

Option 3 — Force link it (not recommended unless you know what you're doing)
brew link postgresql@17 --force
💥 Warning: This can override other PostgreSQL versions.

✔ Summary
Homebrew installed PostgreSQL 17 but didn’t put it in your main /opt/homebrew/bin PATH because it’s marked as keg-only. You can still use it — you just need to call it explicitly or add it to PATH.

If you want, I can help you verify which version is active, or configure PostgreSQL 17 as your default.

Du sade:
Explain symlinks. Also my local repository look good but my remote repository has a folder that I want to delete. How to sync remote and local?
ChatGPT sade:
Sure — I’ll explain symlinks first, then show you how to sync your remote repo with your local repo so the extra folder on GitHub is deleted.

🪢 1. What Symlinks Are (Simple Explanation)
A symlink (symbolic link) is like a shortcut or alias to another file or directory.

It looks like a file.

It points to another location.

When you open it or run it, the system uses the real file.

Example:
If you have:

/opt/homebrew/Cellar/postgresql@17/bin/psql
Homebrew might create a symlink:

/opt/homebrew/bin/psql  →  ../Cellar/postgresql@17/bin/psql
So when you type:

psql
your system automatically runs the file it points to.

Symlinks are useful because:

You can switch versions without changing your PATH

Tools don’t need to know the real file’s deep directory

Homebrew can manage multiple versions cleanly

🔄 2. Your Git Situation: Local looks good, but remote (GitHub) has an extra folder
This means your local branch and remote branch are not in sync.
Usually the remote folder exists because:

It was created in GitHub web UI, or

It was created in a different clone, or

It wasn’t deleted locally and pushed yet.

✅ How to delete the folder on the remote so it matches your local repo
If the folder does not exist locally
Then simply do a normal commit and push from your local machine:

Step 1 — Pull latest changes
git pull
(Resolve merge conflicts if any.)

Step 2 — Stage the deletion
Git automatically marks files/folders missing from your working directory as deleted:

git add -A
Step 3 — Commit
git commit -m "Remove unused folder"
Step 4 — Push
git push
After pushing, GitHub will delete the folder.

🧹 If the folder does exist locally but you want it gone
Just delete it locally:

rm -rf foldername
git add -A
git commit -m "Delete folder"
git push
GitHub will now remove it from the remote.

🛠️ Alternative: Delete folder directly from GitHub GUI
You can delete every file inside the folder on GitHub by:

Opening each file

Clicking the trash can icon

Committing changes

When the folder becomes empty, GitHub removes it.
(This is slower, but works.)

🔁 If local and remote diverged too much
You can force your local state to overwrite the remote:

⚠️ Danger: This overwrites remote history. Only use if you're sure.

git push --force
👍 Summary
✔ Symlinks are “shortcuts” that point to real files
✔ Your local repo is the source of truth
✔ Push deletions from local to remote using:
git add -A
git commit -m "Remove folder"
git push
If you want, I can walk you through:

Checking which branch you’re on

Verifying whether the folder exists locally

Cleaning up the remote safely without force-pushing

Just tell me your situation!

