Ah — that makes sense. What you’re seeing is a combination of case-insensitive filesystems (like macOS’s default HFS+/APFS) and how Git tracks file paths. Let me explain carefully.

🔹 Why Scripts doesn’t appear when you pull
Git stores file paths case-sensitively, but your local macOS filesystem is case-insensitive by default.

On GitHub remote, the folder is Scripts.

Locally, you already have scripts (or no folder at all).

Git sees no “change” on pull because your filesystem maps Scripts and scripts to the same path.

Git cannot create Scripts alongside scripts on a case-insensitive filesystem.

So git fetch / git pull won’t create the old folder — Git thinks your working tree is already correct.

Result:

The folder exists on GitHub (Scripts).

Locally, you have scripts or nothing.

Pulling does not fetch Scripts, because the local filesystem cannot distinguish it from scripts.

✅ How to fix this
You need to force Git to recognize the rename, like we discussed before:

Option 1 — Temporary rename
# Rename to temporary name
git mv Scripts tempfolder
git commit -m "Temporary rename"

# Rename to lowercase
git mv tempfolder scripts
git commit -m "Rename Scripts → scripts"
git push
This will replace Scripts on GitHub with scripts. After that, git pull will behave normally.

