What does install location mean? # Build package correctly
          pkgbuild \
          --root payload/usr/local/pwss_installer \
          --install-location /usr/local/pwss_installer \
          --scripts payload/usr/local/pwss_installer/scripts \
          --identifier com.pwss.integrityhash \
          --version 1.0.0 \
          IntegrityHashInstaller.pkg
ChatGPT sade:
Ah! Let’s break this down carefully. In your pkgbuild command, the --install-location is a very important concept in macOS package building.

🔹 What --install-location means
--install-location <path> tells the installer where to place the files on the target system when the user runs the .pkg.

It is the absolute path on the end-user machine, not the path on your local build machine.

During packaging, pkgbuild maps files from your local --root folder to this install location.

Example from your command:
--root payload/usr/local/pwss_installer
--install-location /usr/local/pwss_installer
--root payload/usr/local/pwss_installer

This is the folder on your build machine that contains the files you want to package.

Think of it as the “source” folder.

--install-location /usr/local/pwss_installer

This is where those files will end up on the user’s machine when the package is installed.

So, anything inside payload/usr/local/pwss_installer locally will be installed to /usr/local/pwss_installer on the Mac where the installer runs.

How scripts are handled:
--scripts payload/usr/local/pwss_installer/scripts
This folder contains preinstall/postinstall scripts.

pkgbuild uses these scripts during installation to run commands relative to the install location.

For example, your postinstall script might do something like:

#!/bin/bash
cd /usr/local/pwss_installer
./install_integrity_hash_macos.sh
It will run after the files are copied to /usr/local/pwss_installer.

✅ TL;DR
--root → where the files are on your build machine

--install-location → where the files will go on the user’s machine

They don’t have to be the same, but often they mirror the same path for convenience.

If you want, I can make a diagram showing how --root maps to --install-location and scripts run, which makes it really intuitive for .pkg building.

Do you want me to do that?

Du sade:
Does that dir need to exit beforehand?
ChatGPT sade:
Nope — the directory specified in --install-location does not need to exist beforehand on the user’s machine.

Here’s how it works:

When the installer runs, macOS creates the directory tree automatically if it doesn’t exist.

All the files from your --root folder are placed there.

If the folder already exists, the files will be added or overwritten according to the package contents.

Example:

pkgbuild \
  --root payload/usr/local/pwss_installer \
  --install-location /usr/local/pwss_installer \
  ...
/usr/local/pwss_installer might not exist on the target Mac.

The installer will create /usr/local/pwss_installer and copy all files from payload/usr/local/pwss_installer there.

💡 Tip:

Make sure your scripts (preinstall/postinstall) handle paths relative to the --install-location.

Don’t assume the folder exists before the installer runs — always reference the install location explicitly.

