Ah — now we see the actual installation failure. Let’s dissect it carefully.

Key part of the log
install:didFailWithError:Error Domain=PKInstallErrorDomain Code=112 "An error occurred while running scripts from the package “IntegrityHashInstaller.pkg”."
UserInfo={NSFilePath=./preinstall, NSURL=file://localhost/private/tmp/IntegrityHashInstaller.pkg, PKInstallPackageIdentifier=com.pwss.integrityhash, NSLocalizedDescription=An error occurred while running scripts from the package “IntegrityHashInstaller.pkg”.}
What it means
PKInstallErrorDomain Code=112

This is a macOS installer error code that specifically means a script in the package failed.

NSFilePath=./preinstall

The installer tried to execute the preinstall script inside your .pkg.

Something in that script caused it to exit with a non-zero status → installer aborts.

Installer stops

macOS installer won’t continue if a preinstall or postinstall script fails.

It gives the generic message:

“The Installer encountered an error that caused the installation to fail.”

Next steps for debugging
Check the preinstall script

Ensure it has correct shebang (first line should be #!/bin/bash or #!/bin/sh).

Make sure it has executable permissions:

chmod +x preinstall
Add logging inside the script

Print messages to a file to see what fails:

#!/bin/bash
echo "Preinstall started" >> /tmp/integrityhash_install.log
# your commands here
echo "Preinstall finished" >> /tmp/integrityhash_install.log
This log is readable even if the installer fails.

Test the script manually

Copy the script to the same path as in the .pkg (payload/usr/local/pwss_installer/scripts/preinstall) and run it in terminal:

sudo ./preinstall
If it fails, the terminal will show the exact error.

Check permissions of files in your payload

All scripts and executables in the .pkg must be readable and executable by root.

✅ TL;DR
Error 112 = script failed (preinstall or postinstall).

Installer didn’t get far — the problem is inside your preinstall script, not the .pkg packaging itself.

Add logging, check permissions, and run the script manually to pinpoint the failure.

